    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my lawn is looking pretty yellow and thin lately. i was looking at some fertilizer at the store and saw stuff labeled 10-10-10 or 20-5-10. what does those numbers actually mean? also how can i tell if my grass is missing phosphorus specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The numbers on fertilizers represent the percentage of three essential nutrients: nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P), and potassium (K). So, 10-10-10 would be 10% nitrogen, 10% phosphorus, and 10% potassium, while 20-5-10 would be 20% nitrogen, 5% phosphorus, and 10% potassium. Phosphorus deficiency in grass is indicated by stunted growth, darker green shade, and delayed maturity. Check your grass for signs of these symptoms to determine if it's lacking phosphorus. You can also conduct a soil test or look at the healthy section of your lawn for a phosphorus content comparison to know if it's correctly balanced.",
